{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787774738,"nonce":"s8nvyWOlDJGNpMUnr0GM9XWP51U=","title":"fix(executor-k8s): make the workspace group explicit, not an fsGroup side effect (b04697f)","message":"\u003e Imported from pull request #79 (MERGED)\n\u003e Branch: `fix/dogfood-run-as-root-workspace-write` → `main`\n\u003e Created: 2026-07-25T18:04:58Z · Merged: 2026-07-25T18:14:33Z\n\nCloses git-bug `b04697f`.\n\nIts literal case — a `run_as_root` step failing on a CAS-restored `/workspace` — landed on `main` in `2fb0b9b` (2026-07-18): after the tar restore, `drive_workspace` runs `chmod -R g+rwX /workspace` plus a `g+s` pass on directories. The comment near `lib.rs:435` is that fix's rationale, not an open TODO. The ticket was simply never closed.\n\nThat path was verified working before anything was changed, by reproducing the DAC model in Docker: a root init container extracts a 65532-owned 0755 tree, the chmod runs, then a step runs `--user 0:0 --cap-drop ALL`. busybox `chmod` does honour `X`, the tree becomes `2775`, and a uid-0 caps-dropped process can write it.\n\nThe step's membership in gid 65532 — the **only** thing letting a caps-dropped step write that group-writable tree — was a *side effect of the Pod-level `fsGroup`*, and a sidecar service is allowed to take it.\n\n`build_pod` assigns `fsGroup` to the first service pinning a non-root `run_as_user` (ADR-0058: the service needs its own data emptyDir chowned) and falls back to 65532 only when no service claims it. So a workspace step declaring:\n\n```yaml\nservices: [{ image: postgres:16, run_as_user: 999 }]\n```\n\nsilently lost the workspace group and hit the identical `Permission denied`.\n\nDocker probe confirming the mechanism: group 65532 → write OK · group 999 only → **Permission denied** · groups {999, 65532} → write OK.\n\n- Named the magic number `WORKSPACE_GID: i64 = 65532`, with the DAC reasoning documented on it.\n- Every workspace Pod now carries `supplementalGroups: [WORKSPACE_GID]` regardless of who won `fsGroup`. `fsGroup` semantics are unchanged — services still win it.\n- Same hole closed in `spawn_debug`, which re-materializes a snapshot into `/workspace` but had **no `fsGroup` at all**: a debug shell got a read-only copy of the workspace it was opened to inspect. Its feed command also gained the `g+s` pass the step path already had. Slightly beyond the ticket's letter — same defect, sibling code path ~80 lines away, 6 lines to fix.\n\n**No privilege widening.** No capability handed back (`drop: ALL`, unprivileged, no-privesc, RuntimeDefault seccomp all still asserted), no ownership change. The step only gains membership in the group that already owns the tree it is meant to build in.\n\n`workspace_steps_are_always_members_of_the_workspace_group` — a pure Pod-spec unit test, no cluster. It asserts the user-visible claim for the `run_as_root` + workspace case, **asserts the ADR-0039 baseline is still intact** so this can't later be \"fixed\" by handing back a capability, and covers the uid-pinning-sidecar regression. Red/green verified: removing `supplementalGroups` fails it.\n\n`cargo test -p scarab-executor-k8s` — 47 passed, 15 ignored (the `SCARAB_TEST_KUBE=1` cluster tier). Integrated with the other three dogfood branches: **597 passed, 21 ignored**.\n\nThe DAC reasoning was validated in Docker and `supplementalGroups` is permitted by the k8s `restricted` PSS profile, but nobody has run a workspace step with a uid-pinning sidecar on colima/kind end-to-end. That belongs in the ignored `SCARAB_TEST_KUBE` tier.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)","files":null}]}